                                            <child>
                                              <object class="GtkComboBoxText" id="Limit_Jack_1_Axis">
                                                <property name="visible">True</property>
                                                <property name="can-focus">False</property>
                                                <property name="tooltip-text" translatable="yes">What the normally-closed limit switch plugged into limit jack 1 protects: both ends of an axis (a switch at each end, wired in series), or only its min (negative) or max (positive) end - use two jacks, one min and one max, when you want LinuxCNC to know which end tripped, e.g. a switch at each end of a curvilinear slide. Only axes assigned to a channel are listed. Choose Not used when nothing is plugged in - an empty jack that's in use reads as a tripped limit. Takes effect the next time LinuxCNC starts.</property>
                                                <property name="halign">center</property>
                                                <property name="valign">center</property>
                                                <signal name="changed" handler="Limit_Jack_1_Axis_Changed" swapped="no"/>
