                                            <child>
                                              <object class="GtkLabel">
                                                <property name="visible">True</property>
                                                <property name="can-focus">False</property>
                                                <property name="halign">start</property>
                                                <property name="valign">start</property>
                                                <property name="label" translatable="yes">Each limit jack takes one limit switch (or one at each end of an axis, wired in series). For each jack choose:
    * &lt;axis&gt; min - the switch at the negative end of travel, where the axis stops when moving in the minus (-) direction,
    * &lt;axis&gt; max - the switch at the positive end of travel, where the axis stops when moving in the plus (+) direction, or
    * &lt;axis&gt; (both ends) - a switch at each end, wired in series into one jack.

Use a min jack and a max jack on the same axis (for example, one at each end of a curvilinear slide) so LinuxCNC knows which end was hit and you can jog back off it. With (both ends), check Override Limits in LinuxCNC to jog off a switch.

Choose Not used for any jack with nothing plugged into it.</property>
                                                <property name="wrap">True</property>
                                                <property name="xalign">0</property>
